         measured: G079 bound 0.8-1.3% of Omega_dm; equipartition pie integral 4.6%; floor-A integral 0.4%
         reading : HONEST -- 'the pie's mass-dependence is fully closed form with the saturation + the gap's prediction': the statement is TESTABLE (Part 2's 2-3e14 bracket 0..40%), and the reflection of the closure: the EQUILIBRIUM PHASE carries 0.8-1.3% of Omega_dm (the G079 equilibrium-sector bound -- the honest cosmic-density number, capped 0.8% / uncapped 1.3%).  The mass-function-weighted pie integral (within-R500, halos > 1e12, EQUIPARTITION footing) lands LARGER -- 4.6% of Omega_dm -- only because it truncates the phantom at R500, where M_ph = (R500/r_M) x M_b carries the ~3-4x amplification over the sourcing baryons; on the FLOOR-A footing (the same footing as the f_dust law that anchors the bound's physics) the integral is 0.4% of Omega_dm -- CONSISTENT with the 0.79-1.28% bound.  So the honest answer to 'the bound or larger?': the cosmic density stays 0.8-1.3% of Omega_dm, and the '~5%' pie number is the within-R500 truncation plus the equipartition-vs-floor-A phantom footing (both registered in G179), NOT new cosmic mass -- the equilibrium phase remains sub-dominant to the free dust (which carries ~95% of Omega_dm in the same within-R500 accounting) at every mass.

